The example meeting (ui/example/fr, ui/example/en) is an invented meeting of two voices, made by
scripts/example.py. No real meeting and no real person are in it.

Synthetic voices generated with ElevenLabs: the model Eleven Multilingual v2, asked through
OpenRouter's speech API on 2026-11-27, speed 0.95, paid per use on OpenRouter. There is no
ElevenLabs subscription. French: the voices « lily » (« Vous ») and « eric » (Hugo). English:
« will » (« Vous ») and « matilda » (Jess).

What the terms say (read on 2026-11-27):
  ElevenLabs, Terms of Service (EEA), last updated 09 October 2026,
  https://elevenlabs.io/terms-of-use-eu: section 4(c)(ii), « as between you and ElevenLabs, you
  retain all rights in and to your Output »; section 1(c), commercial purposes for access « through
  a paid subscription plan », non-commercial purposes only otherwise; section 18 covers a
  subscription bought from « an authorized ElevenLabs reseller or partner ».
  OpenRouter, Terms of Service, last updated 31 August 2026, https://openrouter.ai/terms: section
  6.1, « Your ownership rights in the Output are set forth in the Model Terms for each Model
  you use ».

What is not established: these sources do not show a right to use this sound commercially.
Speech paid per use on OpenRouter is no ElevenLabs subscription, nothing shows OpenRouter as an
authorised reseller under section 18, and the model terms OpenRouter refers to for ElevenLabs were
not readable.

The decider accepted that risk on 2026-11-27, the sound being a demonstration bundled with
Kitokey and not sold as such. Should ElevenLabs or OpenRouter object, these clips are replaced by
an engine whose rights are established.

Changes: the service's speech is resampled to 24,000 samples a second, laid out on two tracks and
kept as FLAC.
